2026-07-29 15:20:36.697 | INFO     | mobile_ws:handle_mobile_ws:204 | 💬 Mobile AI prompt received: '# JARVIS — Fix the 3D Face (No Facial Features) + Consolidate Top Nav Into a Dropdown  ## Problem 1: The 3D face has no face on it  What shipped is a plain wireframe ellipsoid ("JARVIS // ACTIVE VOICE NODE" label above it) — it has **zero facial features**: no eyes, no mouth, no nose, no brows. It's a glowing egg-shaped mesh, not a face. Compare this directly against the reference image I gave earlier, which showed an actual humanoid face — clear eyes, a recognizable mouth/jaw region, a nose bridge, particle/wireframe styling layered *on top of* real facial geometry, not replacing it.  This means whatever was built for "3D Face" mode skipped the actual face model/blendshapes work entirely and just rendered a generic primitive shape with the JARVIS glow effect applied — the amplitude-sync and speaking-state UI around it (the "SPEECH AMPLITUDE SYNC" label, "JARVIS SPEAKING" indicator) may be real, but the model it's driving has nothing to animate.  **Fix:** 1. Actually bring in a real face mesh with distinct eyes, mouth, nose, and brow regions — either a proper rigged model (Ready Player Me export, as discussed previously) or, if a photorealistic avatar doesn't fit the existing cyan-wireframe HUD aesthetic, a **stylized wireframe/particle face that still has clearly defined eye and mouth geometry** — e.g., two distinct glowing eye shapes that can blink/narrow, and a mouth region built from geometry that can actually open/close — not a blank shape with no articulated parts at all. Either direction is fine, but "an unadorned ellipsoid" is neither. 2. The mouth region specifically needs actual open/close geometry (even in Phase 2's amplitude-driven approach from the last prompt) — if there's no mouth-shaped geometry on the model in the first place, amplitude-driven animation has nothing to visibly move. Confirm the model has a jaw/mouth blendshape or separate mesh piece before wiring audio-reactivity to it. 3. Same for eyes — add blink capability (even a simple scale/close animation on a distinct eye shape) so the face doesn't look frozen and lifeless while "speaking." 4. **Test by comparing a screenshot of the result directly against the reference image** — if it still doesn't show a recognizable eyes/nose/mouth arrangement, it's not done, regardless of how the amplitude sync or glow effects look.  ## Problem 2: Consolidate the top nav into a dropdown  Currently the top bar has seven separate always-visible buttons (Command Center, Workflow Studio, Task Queue, Telemetry, Live Mode, Automation, Debug Inspector) plus the right-side controls (SERIOUS MODE, Palette, trash icon, sliders icon). Replace the seven left-side section tabs with a single dropdown/menu button that expands to show and let you pick between them, instead of all seven sitting permanently in the header. Keep the current active section's name visible on the collapsed dropdown button itself (e.g. "☰ Command Center ▾") so it's clear which section you're in without needing to open the menu. Keep the right-side controls (SERIOUS MODE, Palette, clear, settings) as-is unless they also feel cluttered once the left side is consolidated — your call, but don't over-collapse things that are already fine.  ## Also apply Phase 0 from the previous prompt here if not already done  If the backend-feature/button audit from the last prompt (real / wired-but-broken / decorative, for every nav section and control) hasn't been completed yet, do it now as part of this pass, since you're touching the nav structure anyway — this is the natural point to also confirm each of these seven sections actually does something real before deciding how prominently to surface it in the new dropdown.  ## Problem 3: Layout — resizable, movable panels with a 70/30 split  Right now the 3D face panel and the chat panel are fixed-size, side by side. Change this to: - **Draggable/resizable panels** — the boundary between the face panel and the chat panel should be a draggable divider (standard resizable-pane pattern), not a fixed split. Persist the user's chosen split ratio (localStorage or a settings file) so it doesn't reset every launch. - **Default layout: 3D face panel ≈70% of the width, chat panel ≈30%** — the face should be the dominant visual element by default, with chat as a supporting sidebar rather than co-equal real estate. - **Everything else (Workflow Studio, Task Queue, Telemetry, Live Mode, Automation, Debug Inspector, and whatever else exists) moves into the nav dropdown from Problem 2, or a collapsible sidebar** — pick whichever fits better once you see it laid out, but the point is: only the face and chat should be permanently on screen by default: everything else is one click away, not competing for space. - Make panels genuinely draggable/repositionable if reasonable given the current layout engine (not just resizable) — if the frontend's current layout approach (flex/grid) makes true drag-and-repositioning a large lift, resizable-with-persisted-ratio is the minimum bar; freely repositionable panels are a nice-to-have on top if it's not disproportionate effort. Tell me honestly which one you actually delivered.  ## Problem 4: Hacker theme  Restyle the whole UI with a "hacker" aesthetic rather than the current cyan-glass HUD look. Concretely, this means: - **Monospace typography throughout** (a real monospace font — e.g. JetBrains Mono, Fira Code, or IBM Plex Mono — not just labeling things "terminal" while keeping a sans-serif font). - **Green or amber phosphor-on-black color scheme** (classic terminal look) as the primary palette — green is the more iconic "hacker" choice (`#00ff41`-ish Matrix green is a reasonable target), used for text, borders, and accents against a near-black background. - **Terminal-style chrome**: thin bordered panels that read like terminal windows, a blinking block cursor in the input field, command-line-style prompts (e.g. a `>` or `$` prefix in the chat input placeholder). - **Subtle scanline/CRT texture overlay** and occasional glitch-style micro-animations (text flicker, brief RGB-split on state changes) used sparingly — tasteful, not overwhelming or distracting during actual use. - The 3D face should still fit this theme — a green-wireframe/particle face reads as more "hacker" than a cyan-glass one, so this pairs naturally with fixing Problem 1's face geometry at the same time. - Keep contrast and legibility a priority — a hacker theme that becomes hard to read chat text against is a regression, not an upgrade.  ## Definition of done (updated)  - A screenshot of "JARVIS SPEAKING" mode shows a recognizable face — distinct eyes, mouth, nose — not a blank mesh shape, compared directly against the reference image. - The mouth visibly opens/closes with audio amplitude (Phase 2 from the previous prompt), and eyes blink periodically. - The seven top nav sections are consolidated into a single dropdown, with the active section still visibly labeled. - The face panel defaults to ~70% width, chat to ~30%, with a working, persisted resizable divider between them. - Everything besides face + chat lives in the dropdown/sidebar, not permanently on screen. - The whole UI reads as a genuine hacker/terminal theme (monospace font, green-on-black, terminal chrome) — not just the old cyan-glass look with a green filter over it. - You've told me plainly whether panels are truly drag-repositionable or just resizable-with-persisted-ratio. - `JARVIS_ISSUES.md` updated honestly, including the Phase 0 audit table if it wasn't already delivered. - Nothing pushed to GitHub until I say so.'
