oundary between the face panel and the chat panel should be a draggable divider (standard resizable-pane pattern), not a fixed split. Persist the user's chosen split ratio (localStorage or a settings file) so it doesn't reset every launch. - **Default layout: 3D face panel ≈70% of the width, chat panel ≈30%** — the face should be the dominant visual element by default, with chat as a supporting sidebar rather than co-equal real estate. - **Everything else (Workflow Studio, Task Queue, Telemetry, Live Mode, Automation, Debug Inspector, and whatever else exists) moves into the nav dropdown from Problem 2, or a collapsible sidebar** — pick whichever fits better once you see it laid out, but the point is: only the face and chat should be permanently on screen by default: everything else is one click away, not competing for space. - Make panels genuinely draggable/repositionable if reasonable given the current layout engine (not just resizable) — if the frontend's current layout approach (flex/grid) makes true drag-and-repositioning a large lift, resizable-with-persisted-ratio is the minimum bar; freely repositionable panels are a nice-to-have on top if it's not disproportionate effort. Tell me honestly which one you actually delivered.  ## Problem 4: Hacker theme  Restyle the whole UI with a "hacker" aesthetic rather than the current cyan-glass HUD look. Concretely, this means: - **Monospace typography throughout** (a real monospace font — e.g. JetBrains Mono, Fira Code, or IBM Plex Mono — not just labeling things "terminal" while keeping a sans-serif font). - **Green or amber phosphor-on-black color scheme** (classic terminal look) as the primary palette — green is the more iconic "hacker" choice (`#00ff41`-ish Matrix green is a reasonable target), used for text, borders, and accents against a near-black background. - **Terminal-style chrome**: thin bordered panels that read like terminal windows, a blinking block cursor in the input field, command-line-style prompts (e.g. a `>` or `$` prefix in the chat input placeholder). - **Subtle scanline/CRT texture overlay** and occasional glitch-style micro-animations (text flicker, brief RGB-split on state changes) used sparingly — tasteful, not overwhelming or distracting during actual use. - The 3D face should still fit this theme — a green-wireframe/particle face reads as more "hacker" than a cyan-glass one, so this pairs naturally with fixing Problem 1's face geometry at the same time. - Keep contrast and legibility a priority — a hacker theme that becomes hard to read chat text against is a regression, not an upgrade.  ## Definition of done (updated)  - A screenshot of "JARVIS SPEAKING" mode shows a recognizable face — distinct eyes, mouth, nose — not a blank mesh shape, compared directly against the reference image. - The mouth visibly opens/closes with audio amplitude (Phase 2 from the previous prompt), and eyes blink periodically. - The seven top nav sections are consolidated into a single dropdown, with the active section still visibly labeled. - The face panel defaults to ~70% width, chat to ~30%, with a working, persisted resizable divider between them. - Everything besides face + chat lives in the dropdown/sidebar, not permanently on screen. - The whole UI reads as a genuine hacker/terminal theme (monospace font, green-on-black, terminal chrome) — not just the old cyan-glass look with a green filter over it. - You've told me plainly whether panels are truly drag-repositionable or just resizable-with-persisted-ratio. - `JARVIS_ISSUES.md` updated honestly, including the Phase 0 audit table if it wasn't already delivered. - Nothing pushed to GitHub until I say so.': Preferred=True
